</ul><h1>Not Heavy Duty</h1><p>The first time we sold these, I thought, <em>Ha! Hammacher Schlemmer? How did we get our hands on THEIR silly products?!</em></p>
<p>Well, the answer turned out to be pretty simple: we were in the process of getting our hands of Hammacher Schlemmer itself.</p>
<p>Funny how that works, huh?</p>
<p>Anyway, I wasn’t the only one amused at the time. <a href="/@kodiak55">@kodiak55</a> wrote:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I remember the Hammacher Schlemmer catalogs from the 80’s and 90’s. IIRC, they had a Rodeo Drive return address and were full of lots of overpriced “luxury” items, seemingly for people who don’t know what to do with all their money.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I include this comment because it’s a great plug for Gnomenclature, the newsletter from our very own Jason Toon documenting some of the highlights (or maybe lowlights) from the Hammacher catalogs throughout the ages. <a href="https://gnomenclature.hammacher.com/">If you haven’t read it, take a look and subscribe</a>.</p>
<p>Anyway, onto the product itself.</p>
<p>From that same sale, we got the following two comments.</p>
<p>From <a href="/@TomDavidson">@TomDavidson</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I’ve got another brand’s version of the mini chainsaw and I have to say that I use it a LOT for quick pruning. It’s considerably faster than a pruning saw (and less physically onerous) and capable of deeper cuts than large clippers.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>And from <a href="/@user73930182">@user73930182</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I got the saw/grinder and it was what I was expecting. Not a heavy-duty tool, light and easy to work with, and worth the price if I can get around six jobs done with it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The gist? These aren’t super intense. If you’ve got a major tree trimming job, you might need something bigger. Or better yet, you should hire a professional. It can be expensive, but so can the hospital bills and/or property damage leveled when you misjudge an angle and drop a huge branch where no one’s expecting it.</p>
<p>But it seems like these would be just fine for some pruning and…</p>
<p>uhh…</p>
<p>other small jobs like… uhh…</p>
<p>Look, I’m just not a very handy person, okay? So why don’t <em>you</em> tell <em>me</em> how you’d use these in the comments? That way, ~I can use your comments to assemble a write-up next time we sell these~ others can make an informed purchasing decision!</p>